#
# Even steps while dragging, and no lurch when the finger lifts.
#
# Three things on top of patch 0009's touch resampling, measured on the
# Jolla Phone with steady 125 Hz drags and 200 ms flings:
#
# - The finger lifting waited for nothing: the moves still queued and the
#   fling's first step landed in one frame, a jump of about three steps on
#   every fling (97, 183, 55 px). The end now waits in the queue behind the
#   moves, which keep being shown frame by frame.
# - Two clocks ticked between frames: the frame's tick (patches 0007, 0011)
#   and the independent timer the touch queue keeps alive. Each advanced the
#   animations by a whole period, so a frame sometimes moved two steps and
#   the next none. While frames come, only a frame's tick moves things on,
#   and drags are resampled at that frame's time on the display's grid, as
#   animations are (the compositor's frame callbacks come 8-14 ms apart).
# - Touch reports arrive late and in bursts (up to 12 ms of varying delay
#   before Pacific's UI thread alone), so a fixed 16 ms behind the frame
#   often had only whole reports to show: 0, 5.6, 11.2 or 16.8 px a frame.
#   The wait now follows the lateness seen lately plus one report interval
#   (16-40 ms; PACIFIC_SCROLL_RESAMPLE_ADAPTIVE=0 keeps it fixed).
#
# Steady drag, step ratio spread: OSNews 0.63 -> 0.31, The Verge 0.78 ->
# 0.42. 200 ms flings: 0.42 -> 0.20. Touch-down to first movement is
# unchanged (42 ms median); while dragging the page trails the finger by
# the adaptive wait.
#
--- a/Source/WebCore/page/scrolling/ThreadedScrollingTree.cpp
